00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
hi  here i am again the natural learning  society what are we going to do with  this thing  one thing we could do is  that anyone who is involved in language  learning  at university in schools in language  schools wherever  please start protesting  if you are forced to learn from  uninteresting content if teachers come  in  and give you  photocopied  news articles that they've selected for  you  if they spend all kinds of time  on explaining points of grammar that you  can't understand and won't be able to  remember  if you are not learning in a natural way  stand up and say i don't want to learn  this way  i want to learn naturally because if i  learn naturally i'm going to learn  better i can learn faster i'm going to  enjoy it  so i think we should start getting some  protest movements going across all the  schools and campuses and elsewhere where  people are being  forced to try to learn and understand  grammatical and theoretical concepts  that the brain is not yet ready to deal  with  and and let me stress that again the  brain will figure out its own rules the  the grammar explanation can help  i'm not saying it doesn't help and and  uh  my uh approach in dealing with grammar  is to buy  simply the smallest  uh the smallest possible  uh grammar book and i occasionally leap  through it and and what i find is that  occasionally leaping through it i see  some things that that remind me of  aspects of the language that i have  already started to observe so that  through naturally absorbing the language  the brain is starting to observe things  but certain other things it doesn't  quite see yet and sometimes these things  have to be pointed out and so leafing  through a grammar book may help you to  notice things and in fact that's the  purpose of correcting we correct things  not because we think that by correcting  something let's say the learner has  written or said that immediately  the learner will then be able to say it  correctly that immediately the brain  will have adjusted and now say those  things correctly that's not how it works  but sometimes correcting something helps  make the learner more  observant and i get back to spitzer he  talked about the three key attitudes  that we need for learning success and  again he emphasized the importance of  input appropriate input meaningful input  he mentioned how the learn the brain is  constantly learning so we know these  things the brain is constantly learning  but the brain needs three things it  needs  a certain degree of alertness in general  and attentiveness to specific issues  that it has to identify because  sometimes they can just go by and they  aren't noticed the second thing is that  the more emotional connection there is  between the learner and the the  subject that they're learning the better  and of course the third thing is  motivation but if we deal with this  alertness a tentative attentiveness it  is certainly true that if you make a  mistake a few times and you're corrected  you start to get a little bit aware  more aware of that particular issue and  so then hopefully you will start to  notice  that structure those words  when you're listening and reading so  you'll say yeah i had trouble expressing  this concept or i used this structure  and it was wrong so let's try and focus  a little more on that particular word or  that particular concept  uh shows up in in my listening  which is hopefully enjoyable because you  selected it  so  the grammar explanations  have to be and the corrections have to  be more along the lines of helping you  to notice things not in the hope that  you will now learn this rule and  therefore get it right  and i should add that a lot of  again in the in the  interest of keeping things natural  and meaningful  we should object to  exercises and drills and we should say  no just give me interesting stories to  read and if you want i will write for  you and i will speak for you and when i  write and speak naturally which is  natural communication you can correct me  if you want not too often please  and this will help make me a little more  attentive to those issues that i'm  struggling with it'll just make certain  words and certain structures stickier in  the brain but the explanation itself is  not going to uh immediately correct me  so when you are confronted with  unnatural learning in your classroom  protest and say i don't want to learn  this way i want to learn the natural way  and i want to join the natural language  society thank you for listening bye for  now

